        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe move to extend `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` and override `handleMethodArgumentNotValid`/`handleExceptionInternal` is the idiomatic Spring approach, and routing framework exceptions through a central hook is a sound, additive design. The problem is that it introduces a **second, parallel implementation of the problem+json skeleton** rather than converging on one.\n\n1. **Duplicated body-shaping logic (`detailBuild` vs `handleExceptionInternal`).** Both methods assemble the identical five-member RFC 7807 skeleton:\n   - `detailBuild` (lines 79\u201387): `setType(url)`, `setTitle(getClass().getSimpleName())`, `setDetail(...)`, `setProperty(\"timestamp\", ...)`, `setProperty(\"schemaValidationErrors\", List.of())`.\n   - `handleExceptionInternal` (lines 207\u2013218): the same `setType`, `setTitle`, default `setDetail`, `timestamp`, and `schemaValidationErrors` \u2014 just guarded by null/contains checks.\n\n   The spec's whole point is a single consistent shape, yet the shape is now defined in two places. A maintainer adding/renaming a member (or changing the title convention) must remember to edit both, and they can silently drift. Extract one shared normalizer (e.g. have `handleExceptionInternal` call a `detailBuild`-style helper, or have the custom `@ExceptionHandler` methods ultimately defer to the same shaping routine) so the skeleton lives in exactly one unit.\n\n2. **Two ways to derive the `type` URI.** `detailBuild` takes a `StringBuffer url` from `request.getRequestURL()`, while the new `requestUrl(WebRequest)` (lines 232\u2013237) re-derives the same value from `ServletWebRequest`. These are two spellings of \"the request URL used as `type`.\" If `detailBuild` accepted an already-resolved URL string, both paths could share `requestUrl` and the `StringBuffer` plumbing would disappear.\n\nNeither finding is about correctness or behavior \u2014 both are purely about collapsing the now-duplicated \"what a problem+json body looks like here\" logic into a single home so the six custom handlers and the framework hook render through one definition."
